  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Lingering spotty rain and snow showers wind down \nfrom west to east through this afternoon, though clouds will linger \nthrough tonight.\n\nAs of late morning some spotty light rain/snow showers and \nsprinkles/flurries persist southeast of the lakes and in favored \nareas of upslope flow...with most of this in liquid form given that \nthe supporting area of low level moisture lies well below the prime \ndendritic snow growth zone. These will gradually wind down from west \nto east through this afternoon as ridging over the Upper Midwest \nbuilds eastward into our region along with increasing subsidence and \ndrying aloft. This being said...this drying will not be enough (yet) \nto scour out the remnant lower clouds...which aside from a few brief \nsunny breaks this morning will hang tough through tonight. Given the \ncloud cover...have again lowered high temps for today as per the \npast couple of days...with the persistent low cloud cover favoring a \nnarrower diurnal range than advertised by blended guidance...with \ntonight's lows again likely to be above this same guidance.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather will return by Monday, with temperatures \ntrending notably warmer by midweek.\n\nAn expansive and strengthening area of surface high will build from \nthe Plains and across the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley today and into \nthe early week. This feature will bring a return to dry conditions, \nwith a few partly to mostly sunny days. Temperatures will remain \ncool ahead of the surface high, but as this surface high slides \neastward, and a stronger southerly flow ensues, afternoon \ntemperatures will rise back into the lower to mid 50s by \nTuesday...and then starting Wednesday a few spots will reach into \nthe 60s, with more and more areas hitting that 60F degree mark by \nthe end of the week.",
